@PLN103 P0.2 — acceptance.expected
Hand-verified per-binding ownership verdicts for acceptance.loft, captured from
`ownership_of` over the committed def.code (2026-07-12, on main + #562) and checked
against semantic truth. This is the ORACLE the P1 `Section::Ownership` overlay must
reproduce. Legend: ✓ = oracle matches semantic truth; ⚠ = a rendering the overlay
must TRANSLATE (a real, defensible oracle output that reads misleadingly raw).

Capture method (reproducible): a temp per-binding dump in `use_analysis::dump_all`
gated on `LOFT_PLN103_DUMP` for `n_k*` fns; run
`LOFT_MATERIALIZE_DUMP=1 LOFT_PLN103_DUMP=1 loft --interpret acceptance.loft`.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
fn k1_borrowed_arm(e: Cell) -> vector<Tok>     return = Join(base=_mvcopy_1)
  Var(e)          = Borrowed(base=e)      ✓  param — borrowed-of-itself (convention)
  Var(_mv_items_1)= Borrowed(base=e)      ✓  KEY: the borrowed field projection survives
                                              on the final IR (P0.1). This is the fact
                                              a naive "classify the delivered result"
                                              overlay would MISS (it reads Owned).
  Var(_mvcopy_1)  = Borrowed(base=_mvcopy_1) ⚠ owned jo copy buffer; self-base — render
                                              as "Owned (backing=_mvcopy_1)".
  Var(__vdb_1)    = Owned                  ✓  buffer
  return          = Join(base=_mvcopy_1)  ✓  owned-copy path vs owned-literal path

fn k2_copy_vs_view(src) -> integer             return = Owned (scalar)
  Var(src)        = Borrowed(base=src)    ✓  param
  Var(b)          = Borrowed(base=__vdb_1) ⚠ `b = src` COPIES; b owns the copy via
                                              __vdb_1 — render "Owned (backing=__vdb_1)".
  Var(first)      = Borrowed(base=b)      ✓  KEY: `b[0]` is a VIEW of b (not a copy) —
                                              the K2 view-vs-copy distinction, correct.

fn k4_emptyarm(v) -> vector<Tok>               return = Borrowed(base=rest)
  Var(v)          = Borrowed(base=v)      ✓  param
  Var(_match_subj_1) = Borrowed(base=v)   ✓  the match subject borrows v
  Var(rest)       = Borrowed(base=rest)   ⚠ `..rest` is a FRESH materialized vector
                                              (owned); self-base — render "Owned".
  Var(_slice_elm_1)= Borrowed(base=_match_subj_1) ✓ a slice element VIEW
  Var(_empty_arm_1)= Borrowed(base=__vdb_1) ✓/⚠ the `[]` arm is a REAL vector (owns
                                              __vdb_1) — confirms the #562 fix; render
                                              "Owned (backing=__vdb_1)" NOT a bare null.

fn k1_owned_or_borrow(pool,i,use_pool) -> Tok  return = Join(base=pool)
  Var(pool)       = Borrowed(base=pool)   ✓  param
  Var(r)          = Join(base=pool)       ✓✓ THE HARD ONE: r is owned (V{n:0}) on the
                                              use_pool=false path, a VIEW of pool on the
                                              true path — a genuine runtime JOIN, and
                                              the oracle identifies it exactly (#316/#495).
  Var(__ncc_1)    = Borrowed(base=pool)   ✓  the ??-temp holds pool[i], a view of pool
  Var(__ref_1/2)  = Owned                 ✓  buffers
  return          = Join(base=pool)       ✓  the join propagates to the return

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FINDINGS FOR P1 (what the overlay must do, learned here):

1. The semantically-important borrows are all CORRECT on the final IR — borrowed
   field projections, element views, and (critically) the runtime JOIN r/return in
   k1_owned_or_borrow. No pre-synthesis snapshot needed (P0.1 confirmed, P0.2 widened
   the evidence to view/copy and runtime-join shapes).

2. THE ONE RENDERING RULE (⚠ rows): an owned buffer/copy classifies as
   `Borrowed(base=X)` where X is the var's OWN backing store — a synthesized buffer
   (`__vdb_*`, `__ref_*`, `_mvcopy_*`) or the var itself (self-base). This is
   defensible (the var's store IS X) but reads as an alias of a live sibling. The
   overlay MUST translate: base ∈ {self, a synthesized buffer name} ⇒ render
   "Owned (backing=X)"; a base naming a USER var/param/other-binding ⇒ a genuine
   "Borrowed(base=X)". This heuristic is the difference between a readable overlay
   and a confusing one, and it belongs in P1.4's renderer, not in `ownership_of`
   (which stays faithful — Out-of-scope §1).

3. `return_ownership` alone is insufficient (it reads Owned/Join at the delivered
   result); the per-binding walk is what exposes the borrowed sources. Confirms the
   A1 per-binding design.
